{"file":{"content":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm,mathtools}\n\\usepackage{enumitem}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{microtype}\n\n\\hypersetup{\n  colorlinks=true,\n  linkcolor=blue!60!black,\n  citecolor=blue!60!black,\n  urlcolor=blue!60!black\n}\n\n\\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}\n\\newtheorem{conjecture}{Conjecture}\n\\newtheorem{problem}{Open Problem}\n\\newtheorem{definition}{Definition}\n\\newtheorem{remark}{Remark}\n\n\\newcommand{\\Z}{\\mathbb Z}\n\\newcommand{\\N}{\\mathbb N}\n\\newcommand{\\E}{\\mathbb E}\n\\newcommand{\\R}{\\mathbb R}\n\\newcommand{\\U}{\\mathcal U}\n\\newcommand{\\Cob}{\\mathrm{Cob}}\n\\newcommand{\\Col}{\\mathrm{Col}}\n\\newcommand{\\ord}{\\operatorname{ord}}\n\\newcommand{\\Spec}{\\operatorname{Spec}}\n\\newcommand{\\supp}{\\operatorname{supp}}\n\\newcommand{\\geom}{\\mathrm{geom}}\n\\newcommand{\\HT}{\\mathcal H}\n\n\\title{The Collatz Obstruction After the Finite-Shadow Reduction\\\\\n\\large What Remains and What New Mathematics Would Be Required}\n\\author{Research note}\n\\date{\\today}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis note summarizes the current end-state of the finite-shadow Collatz program developed in the accompanying manuscripts and computational explorations.  The main conclusion is not that the Collatz conjecture has been proved.  Rather, the obstruction has been compressed into a precise form.  The finite annealed side is controlled by a non-self-adjoint twisted transfer-operator theory: coboundaries are classified, non-coboundary finite shadows have a uniform spectral gap at $s=0$, and the previously open cascade and flattening estimates have been reduced to explicit arithmetic-energy statements.  The global pointwise side is reduced by the keystone arrow: any bad positive Syracuse orbit must cast a finite non-coboundary two-point shadow.  Cycles, low-complexity calibrated ghosts, and bounded-discrepancy symbolic candidates are excluded by a combination of $2$-adic coding rigidity, max-plus descent certificates, and Baker/linear-forms bounds.  What remains is a joint $2$-/ $3$-adic deterministic rigidity problem: rule out positive-realizable, high-complexity, mean-critical, non-coboundary-biased traps that avoid every mixed-tower max-plus certificate.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\tableofcontents\n\n\\section{Accelerated Syracuse notation}\n\nWe work with the odd accelerated Syracuse map\n\\[\n  T(x)=\\frac{3x+1}{2^{b(x)}},\n  \\qquad b(x)=v_2(3x+1),\n\\]\nwhere $x$ is odd and positive unless otherwise stated.  Along an orbit\n\\[\n  x_0,x_1,x_2,\\ldots,\n\\]\nwrite\n\\[\n  b_n=b(x_n),\n  \\qquad B_N=\\sum_{n<N}b_n.\n\\]\nThe critical slope is\n\\[\n  \\log_2 3.\n\\]\nThe logarithmic identity is\n\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:log-identity}\n  \\log_2 x_{N}\n  =\n  \\log_2 x_0\n  +N\\log_2 3-B_N\n  +\\sum_{n<N}\\log_2\\left(1+\\frac{1}{3x_n}\\right).\n\\end{equation}\nThus mean letter excess\n\\[\n  \\frac{1}{N}B_N>\\log_2 3+\\eta\n\\]\nforces descent once the positive correction term in \\eqref{eq:log-identity} is dominated.  This is the elementary drift mechanism behind the C1 part of the program.\n\nFor a finite character $\\chi'$ modulo $3^r$, define the two-point shadow\n\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:shadow}\n  F_{\\chi'}(x_n,x_{n+1})\n  =\\chi'(x_{n+1})\\overline{\\chi'(3x_n+1)}.\n\\end{equation}\nSince\n\\[\n  x_{n+1}=\\frac{3x_n+1}{2^{b_n}},\n\\]\nwe have the exact identity\n\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:shadow-letter}\n  F_{\\chi'}(x_n,x_{n+1})=\\chi'(2)^{-b_n}.\n\\end{equation}\nThe geometric stationary value for $z=\\chi'(2)$ is\n\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:geom-value}\n  G(z)=\\E_{\\geom}[z^{-b}]\n  =\\sum_{b\\ge1}2^{-b}z^{-b}\n  =\\frac{z^{-1}/2}{1-z^{-1}/2}.\n\\end{equation}\n\n\\section{What has been reduced to finite spectral theory}\n\nThe finite side of the program studies synchronized residue/letter transfer operators twisted by characters.  The precise operator depends on the chosen finite level, strip/killing convention, and tilt, but the structural conclusions are stable.\n\n\\subsection{Coboundary classification}\n\nThe first structural theorem is the finite coboundary classification:\n\\[\n  \\Cob=\\langle\\chi_2\\rangle,\n\\]\nwhere $\\chi_2$ is the quadratic/parity character.  The proof uses branch comparison, especially the $b$ versus $b+2$ relation, and the fact that $2$ is a primitive root modulo powers of $3$.  Its consequence is that the only directions in which the twisted transfer operator can be peripheral are the trivial direction and the parity coboundary direction.\n\nThis theorem is load-bearing throughout the program.  It separates genuine non-coboundary shadows from the parity escape, and it is what makes the keystone arrow finite rather than infinite-dimensional.\n\n\\subsection{Finite spectral gap at $s=0$}\n\nThe finite operator theorem at $s=0$ can be summarized as follows.\n\n\\begin{theorem}[Finite non-coboundary spectral gap, informal]\nFor each finite conductor level, every primitive non-coboundary twisted synchronized operator has spectral radius strictly below the untwisted positive operator.  Moreover, after the cascade, flattening, and class-IV estimates are assembled, the high-conductor sector has a uniform gap at $s=0$.\n\\end{theorem}\n\nThe proof was not a single spectral estimate.  It required several reductions:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}[label=(\\alph*)]\n  \\item exact coboundary classification;\n  \\item conductor collapse and descent for primitive sectors;\n  \\item pair-correlation phase formula and exact nonstationary vanishing;\n  \\item threshold cascade contraction;\n  \\item bounded-shell flattening for the renewal constants;\n  \\item one-condition degeneracy cost for class IV;\n  \\item cellwise harmonic analysis for the remaining degenerate sums.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nThe important conceptual point is that the finite gap is an \\emph{annealed} result.  It says that the transfer operator mixes non-coboundary shadows at a uniform rate under the finite branch/residue dynamics.  It does not by itself say that a single deterministic positive orbit realizes this mixing.\n\n\\section{The keystone arrow}\n\nThe global reduction is the keystone arrow.\n\n\\begin{theorem}[Keystone arrow, informal]\nIf a positive Syracuse orbit is bad, meaning divergent or eventually trapped in a nontrivial positive cycle, then for some finite $r$ and some non-coboundary character $\\chi'$ modulo $3^r$, the empirical averages of the two-point shadow\n\\[\n  \\chi'(x_{n+1})\\overline{\\chi'(3x_n+1)}\n\\]\nfail to converge to the geometric stationary value \\eqref{eq:geom-value}.\n\\end{theorem}\n\nThe proof has three ingredients.\n\nFirst, a bad orbit has letter mean below the stationary geometric mean $2$; in the divergent case this follows from distinctness and a summability estimate, while in the nontrivial cycle case it follows from the cycle drift inequality.  Equality is reserved for the trivial cycle.\n\nSecond, by \\eqref{eq:shadow-letter}, letter characters are exactly two-point shadows.\n\nThird, if every non-coboundary finite shadow were stationary, the remaining possible deviation would be parity-only.  The parity deviation spreads over residues modulo $2\\cdot3^j$ with pointwise correction tending to zero.  Tightness then forces the full letter law to be geometric, hence mean $2$, contradicting the drift conclusion.\n\n\\begin{remark}[One-directional nature]\nThe arrow is one-directional.  The trivial cycle itself has $b_n\\equiv2$, hence\n\\[\n  \\frac1N\\sum_{n<N}F_{\\chi'}(x_n,x_{n+1})=\\chi'(2)^{-2},\n\\]\nwhich is generally not equal to the geometric value \\eqref{eq:geom-value}.  Thus the statement ``every orbit has stationary non-coboundary shadows'' is false whenever the Collatz conjecture is true.  The corrected endpoint is exclusion of persistent non-coboundary shadows on non-eventually-trivial positive orbits, or a proof that such shadows force descent.\n\\end{remark}\n\n\\section{What has been excluded}\n\nThe current reductions eliminate many natural ghost/counterexample classes.\n\n\\subsection{Cycles}\n\nFor a periodic valuation word $w=(b_0,\\ldots,b_{p-1})$, let\n\\[\n  B=\\sum_{i=0}^{p-1}b_i,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n  A_w=\\sum_{j=0}^{p-1}3^{p-1-j}2^{B_j},\n  \\qquad B_j=\\sum_{i<j}b_i.\n\\]\nThe corresponding affine iterate is\n\\[\n  T_w(x)=\\frac{3^p x+A_w}{2^B}.\n\\]\nA cycle satisfies\n\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:cycle-point}\n  x=\\frac{A_w}{2^B-3^p}.\n\\end{equation}\nBaker/Rhin-type lower bounds for linear forms in two logarithms applied to\n\\[\n  B\\log 2-p\\log 3\n\\]\ngive an effective upper bound on the minimum element of a hypothetical cycle in terms of $p$.  Together with verified lower ranges, this excludes periodic traps up to corresponding bounds.  Conceptually, the linear form in logarithms is the archimedean max-plus certificate for closed residue traps.\n\n\\subsection{Low-complexity aperiodic ghosts}\n\nA key discovery is $2$-adic repetition rigidity.  If two positions in an orbit carry the same length-$p$ valuation block $u$, and\n\\[\n  B_u=\\sum_{i<p}u_i,\n\\]\nthen the two starting points lie in a single residue class modulo $2^{B_u+1}$, in particular at least modulo $2^{p+1}$.  Hence, inside a band\n\\[\n  x_n\\in[Y,WY],\n\\]\nif\n\\[\n  2^{p+1}>Y(W-1),\n\\]\nthen two equal length-$p$ blocks force the two starting points to be equal.  A genuine integer cycle is manufactured on the nose.\n\nConsequently, a banded orbit segment of length $M$ at height $Y$ must have nearly full factor diversity at scale\n\\[\n  p\\approx\\log_2 Y,\n\\]\nunless it contains a cycle.  Combining this with the Baker cycle bound gives a complexity-height tradeoff: long banded no-descent stretches at high height force high word complexity.\n\nThis excludes Sturmian, Beatty, morphic, linearly recurrent, polynomial-complexity, and bounded-discrepancy candidate valuation words.  In particular, the earlier natural candidate family\n\\[\n  b_n\\in\\{1,2\\},\n  \\qquad \\Pr(b=1)=2-\\log_2 3,\n\\]\nwith Sturmian balance is a $2$-adic ghost class, not a positive counterexample class.\n\n\\subsection{Mean-visible deviations}\n\nIf a window has\n\\[\n  \\frac1M\\sum_{n=N}^{N+M-1}b_n\\ge\\log_2 3+\\eta\n\\]\nwith\n\\[\n  \\eta>\\frac{1}{3x_N\\ln2},\n\\]\nthen \\eqref{eq:log-identity} forces descent within the window.  Therefore any surviving counterexample must be phase-only: near-critical in mean, but biased in some non-coboundary finite shadow.\n\n\\section{The remaining obstruction}\n\nThe remaining obstruction can now be stated precisely.\n\n\\begin{problem}[Joint-tower max-plus rigidity]\nShow that every positive-realizable, descent-free, non-coboundary-biased trap coherent in the mixed tower $2^a3^b$ admits a finite max-plus descent certificate at some mixed level.\n\\end{problem}\n\nMore explicitly, a trap $H$ in a finite mixed residue graph carries two labels:\n\\[\n  g(b)=\\log_2 3-b,\n\\]\nand, for a chosen non-coboundary character and phase,\n\\[\n  \\phi(b)=\\Re\\left(\\omega\\left(\\chi'(2)^{-b}-G(\\chi'(2))\\right)\\right).\n\\]\nA max-plus certificate consists of $\\lambda\\ge0$, a potential $\\Psi$, and $\\delta>0$ such that for every edge $a\\xrightarrow{b}a'$ in the trap,\n\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:certificate}\n  g(b)+\\lambda(\\phi(b)-\\varepsilon)+\\Psi(a')-\\Psi(a)\\le -\\delta.\n\\end{equation}\nSumming \\eqref{eq:certificate} along an orbit segment and using \\eqref{eq:log-identity} forces descent once the segment is long relative to $\\operatorname{osc}(\\Psi)/\\delta$.\n\nBy max-plus duality, such a certificate exists precisely when the maximum cycle mean of\n\\[\n  g+\\lambda(\\phi-\\varepsilon)\n\\]\nfalls below zero for some $\\lambda\\ge0$.  This is finite and Karp-computable at each fixed mixed level.  What is missing is the deterministic rigidity theorem saying that a positive-realizable coherent trap must eventually admit such a certificate.\n\n\\subsection{Why $3$-adic information alone is insufficient}\n\nThe $3$-adic-only version is false.  The all-ones trap $b\\equiv1$ is coherent modulo every power of $3$, biased, certificate-free, and descent-free in the $3$-adic graph.  It is eliminated only by the $2$-adic tower: $b\\equiv1$ forever forces\n\\[\n  x\\equiv -1\\pmod{2^k}\n\\]\nfor every $k$, hence $x=-1$ in $\\Z_2$.  This is the negative $-1$ ghost, not a positive counterexample.\n\nThus the $2$-adic realization tower is not auxiliary.  It supplies the archimedean/positivity obstruction invisible to the $3$-adic shadow graph.\n\n\\section{The surviving counterexample profile}\n\nAfter the reductions above, a counterexample, if one exists, must be a valuation sequence satisfying all of the following:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}[label=(\\roman*)]\n  \\item \\textbf{Mean-criticality:}\n  \\[\n    \\frac1N\\sum_{n<N}b_n\\approx\\log_2 3,\n  \\]\n  so C1 descent does not apply.\n\n  \\item \\textbf{Persistent non-coboundary finite shadow bias:}\n  for some finite non-coboundary $\\chi'$,\n  \\[\n    \\frac1N\\sum_{n<N}\\chi'(2)^{-b_n}\n    \\not\\to G(\\chi'(2)).\n  \\]\n\n  \\item \\textbf{High factor complexity in every long banded window:}\n  for $p\\sim\\log_2Y$ inside $x_n\\in[Y,WY]$, the length-$p$ factor complexity must be nearly maximal.  Otherwise repetition rigidity manufactures a cycle, which Baker excludes.\n\n  \\item \\textbf{Mixed-adic coherence:}\n  the word must be coherent simultaneously in the $2$-adic realization tower and the $3$-adic shadow tower.\n\n  \\item \\textbf{Certificate resistance:}\n  no finite mixed-level max-plus certificate may appear.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\nThus the surviving profile is not low-complexity or Sturmian.  It is a high-complexity, randomness-mimicking, phase-biased, mixed-adically coherent object.  This is a narrow class, but it is precisely where the deterministic-equidistribution barrier remains.\n\n\\section{What new mathematics is required}\n\nThe remaining step is not another ordinary spectral estimate.  The finite annealed spectral theory has already been proved.  The missing mathematics must connect finite annealed rigidity to a single deterministic positive orbit.\n\n\\subsection{Mixed-tower max-plus rigidity}\n\nThe first required tool is a projective max-plus rigidity theorem.\n\n\\begin{conjecture}[Coherent lift certificate principle]\nLet $H_{a,b}$ be a projectively coherent family of traps in mixed residue graphs modulo $2^a3^b$, with persistent non-coboundary bias and no mean-visible descent.  If the family is positive-realizable by integer orbit segments, then for some $(a,b)$ it admits a max-plus certificate of the form \\eqref{eq:certificate}, unless it is the trivial cycle.\n\\end{conjecture}\n\nThis is a deterministic substitute for annealed spectral mixing.  It says that failure of randomness along one orbit must create finite algebraic structure, and that such structure is either a ghost, a coboundary, or descent-forcing.\n\n\\subsection{$S$-unit/subspace rigidity for high-complexity banded windows}\n\nFor a block of length $p$ beginning at $n$, one has\n\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:block-identity}\n  x_{n+p}\n  =\\frac{3^p x_n+A_{n,p}}{2^{B_{n,p}}},\n\\end{equation}\nwhere\n\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:sunit-sum}\n  A_{n,p}=\\sum_{j=0}^{p-1}3^{p-1-j}2^{B_{n,j}}.\n\\end{equation}\nEquivalently,\n\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:sunit-relation}\n  2^{B_{n,p}}x_{n+p}-3^p x_n=A_{n,p}.\n\\end{equation}\nThe terms in \\eqref{eq:sunit-sum} are $\\{2,3\\}$-unit monomials.  A high-complexity banded window supplies many distinct relations of the form \\eqref{eq:sunit-relation} with $x_n,x_{n+p}$ lying in a short multiplicative interval.\n\nThe desired new theorem is a subspace-theorem type statement:\n\n\\begin{conjecture}[Banded $S$-unit rigidity]\nToo many coherent relations of the form \\eqref{eq:sunit-relation}, with $x_n,x_{n+p}$ confined to a multiplicative band and with high factor complexity, force the exponent vectors appearing in \\eqref{eq:sunit-sum} into finitely many linear patterns.  Those patterns yield repeated/effectively periodic blocks or a max-plus certificate.\n\\end{conjecture}\n\nThis would handle the high-complexity banded-window regime.  It is the natural successor to the periodic Baker argument: closed cycles give one sharp two-logarithm relation; aperiodic high-complexity windows give many $S$-unit relations.\n\n\\subsection{Band-crossing certificates}\n\nA counterexample might try to avoid long banded windows entirely.  Then it must cross multiplicative bands quickly while avoiding descent.  Such rapid upward crossing requires a high density of low letters, especially $b=1$.  But persistent $b=1$ behavior is precisely where the $2$-adic tower forces ghosts such as $x=-1$.\n\nThe needed theorem is:\n\n\\begin{conjecture}[Band-crossing certificate]\nIf a positive Syracuse orbit avoids long banded windows while remaining mean-critical and phase-biased, then at some mixed level $2^a3^b$ the induced trap admits a max-plus descent certificate, unless it converges to a nonpositive $2$-adic ghost.\n\\end{conjecture}\n\nTogether, the banded $S$-unit theorem and the band-crossing certificate would cover the two remaining escape routes.\n\n\\subsection{Aperiodic Baker technology}\n\nThe periodic Baker argument uses the closed relation\n\\[\n  B\\log2-p\\log3\\approx0.\n\\]\nFor an aperiodic orbit segment, \\eqref{eq:block-identity} gives\n\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:aperiodic-linear-form}\n  B_{n,p}\\log2-p\\log3\n  =\n  \\log\\frac{x_n}{x_{n+p}}\n  +\n  \\log\\left(1+\\frac{A_{n,p}}{3^p x_n}\\right).\n\\end{equation}\nA new ``Baker along orbits'' theorem would produce sufficiently sharp near-return blocks for which the left side of \\eqref{eq:aperiodic-linear-form} is too small relative to known lower bounds for linear forms in logarithms, or would use many moderate block relations through $S$-unit/subspace methods.\n\nThis is not presently standard linear-forms technology.  It is a hybrid of:\n\\[\n  \\text{linear forms in logarithms}\n  +\n  \\text{ergodic optimization/calibrated measures}\n  +\n  \\text{symbolic complexity}\n  +\n  \\text{mixed-adic realizability}.\n\\]\n\n\\section{Interpretation}\n\nThe physical/spectral picture is now clearer.  The Collatz system has not been placed into a self-adjoint Hamiltonian or Selberg-trace framework.  The natural finite object is instead non-self-adjoint: a twisted transfer operator with arithmetic phases, killed/strip dynamics, and max-plus descent certificates.  The self-adjoint objects $T^*T$ are norm-control auxiliaries, not fundamental Hamiltonians.\n\nThe final obstruction is therefore not a missing eigenvalue computation.  It is a deterministic rigidity problem: show that no single positive integer orbit can realize the high-complexity, mixed-adic, non-coboundary-biased traps that remain after all annealed and low-complexity mechanisms have been eliminated.\n\n\\section{Current endpoint}\n\nThe program has reached the following precise endpoint.\n\n\\begin{center}\n\\fbox{\\begin{minipage}{0.92\\textwidth}\nAny counterexample must be a positive-realizable valuation sequence that is mean-critical, non-coboundary shadow-biased, high-complexity inside every long banded window, coherent in the mixed $2^a3^b$ tower, and resistant to every finite max-plus descent certificate.  Proving Collatz in this framework requires a new deterministic rigidity theorem excluding such objects, most plausibly by combining banded $S$-unit/subspace rigidity with mixed-tower max-plus certificates.\n\\end{minipage}}\n\\end{center}\n\nThis is the remaining obstruction in its sharpest current form.\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{9}\n\n\\bibitem{Baker}\nA. Baker, \\emph{Linear forms in the logarithms of algebraic numbers}, Mathematika 13 (1966), 204--216.\n\n\\bibitem{Bugeaud}\nY. Bugeaud, \\emph{Linear Forms in Logarithms and Applications}, IRMA Lectures in Mathematics and Theoretical Physics, European Mathematical Society, 2018.\n\n\\bibitem{EvertseSchlickeweiSchmidt}\nJ.-H. Evertse, H. P. Schlickewei, and W. M. Schmidt, Linear equations in variables which lie in a multiplicative group, \\emph{Annals of Mathematics} 155 (2002), 807--836.\n\n\\bibitem{SimonsDeWeger}\nJ. L. Simons and B. M. M. de Weger, Theoretical and computational bounds for $m$-cycles of the $3n+1$ problem, \\emph{Acta Arithmetica} 117 (2005), 51--70.\n\n\\bibitem{Tao}\nT. Tao, Almost all orbits of the Collatz map attain almost bounded values, \\emph{Forum of Mathematics, Pi} 10 (2022), e12.\n\n\\bibitem{Jenkinson}\nO. Jenkinson, Ergodic optimization in dynamical systems, \\emph{Ergodic Theory and Dynamical Systems} 39 (2019), 2593--2618.\n\n\\bibitem{AdamczewskiBugeaud}\nB. Adamczewski and Y. Bugeaud, On the complexity of algebraic numbers I. Expansions in integer bases, \\emph{Annals of Mathematics} 165 (2007), 547--565.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n","filePath":"/home/john/mynotes/shadow_frontier/collatz_obstruction_new_math.tex","numLines":429,"startLine":1,"totalLines":429},"type":"text"}